rsz: restore the design area from the journal instead of recounting every instance

Per-pass cost fix from the repair-knobs campaign (ideas/repair-knobs-nihl.md,
third study). Not upstreamed; carried while it is measured. Changes no
result: the area after an undo is the area at the checkpoint.

Every time the setup sweep gives up on an endpoint it restores the ECO
journal, and MoveCommitter::restoreJournal begins with
Resizer::initForJournalRestore(), which is Resizer::init(): a recount of
the design area over every instance of the block, a levelization check,
and a flush of the swappable-cell cache. The per-visit trace of the
default (patches/0078, 0066) puts journal restore at 14-36% of the setup
phase (ibex floorplan: 25 s of 71, 480 restores), and the recount is the
part that scales with the design rather than with the undo.

The design area is tracked incrementally by designAreaIncr on every move;
an ECO undo does not reverse those increments, which is why init()
recounted. This patch snapshots the tracked area when a journal level
opens and puts it back when the level is undone, exactly, in O(1); the
restore keeps the levelization check and stops flushing the
swappable-cell cache, whose contents depend on the libraries only.

Chesterton's Fence: the recount made the area right after an undo; the
snapshot makes it right for the same reason the undo itself is right.
diff --git a/src/rsz/include/rsz/Resizer.hh b/src/rsz/include/rsz/Resizer.hh
index 86a6e4ff73..a73f30d664 100644
--- a/src/rsz/include/rsz/Resizer.hh
+++ b/src/rsz/include/rsz/Resizer.hh
@@ -402,6 +402,12 @@ class Resizer : public sta::dbStaState, public sta::dbNetworkObserver
   double designArea();
   // Increment design_area
   void designAreaIncr(float delta);
+  // The tracked design area as it stands, without a recount, and a way to
+  // put it back: the journal snapshots it when a search branch opens and
+  // restores it when the branch is undone, so an undo costs no walk of
+  // every instance.
+  double trackedDesignArea() const { return design_area_; }
+  void setTrackedDesignArea(double area) { design_area_ = area; }
   // Caller owns return value.
   sta::NetSeq* findFloatingNets();
   sta::PinSet* findFloatingPins();
diff --git a/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.cc b/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.cc
index fa93ecd3cf..a7075c35e3 100644
--- a/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.cc
+++ b/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.cc
@@ -489,6 +489,7 @@ void MoveCommitter::beginJournal()
   // that with a fresh per-level result vector here.
   beginEcoJournal();
   pending_move_results_.emplace();
+  journal_design_area_.push(resizer_.trackedDesignArea());
   // Mirror the journal level in the tracker so a nested restore rejects only
   // its own moves.
   if (move_tracker_) {
@@ -516,6 +517,8 @@ void MoveCommitter::commitJournal()
 
   std::vector<MoveResult> top = std::move(pending_move_results_.top());
   pending_move_results_.pop();
+  // The committed moves' area stays; only the snapshot for this level goes.
+  journal_design_area_.pop();
 
   if (pending_move_results_.empty()) {
     // Outermost commit: fold the accumulated pending counters into the
@@ -551,6 +554,9 @@ void MoveCommitter::restoreJournal()
 
   const bool had_eco_changes = ecoHasPendingChanges();
   restoreEcoJournal();
+  // The netlist is back at this level's checkpoint, and so is its area.
+  resizer_.setTrackedDesignArea(journal_design_area_.top());
+  journal_design_area_.pop();
   // Mirror the journal restore in the tracker so its moves are rejected,
   // whether this is an outermost or a nested restore.
   if (move_tracker_) {
diff --git a/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.hh b/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.hh
index e2cd65c173..468ea72418 100644
--- a/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.hh
+++ b/src/rsz/src/MoveCommitter.hh
@@ -168,6 +168,8 @@ class MoveCommitter
   // unrecord just its own moves and a nested commit can merge its results into
   // the parent's vector (mirroring odb's commitEco append-to-parent semantics).
   std::stack<std::vector<MoveResult>> pending_move_results_;
+  // The tracked design area when each journal level opened.
+  std::stack<double> journal_design_area_;
   std::array<int, kTypeCount> pending_by_type_{};
   int netlist_edits_ = 0;
   std::array<int, kTypeCount> committed_by_type_{};
diff --git a/src/rsz/src/Resizer.cc b/src/rsz/src/Resizer.cc
index 3e11793881..557b7a7634 100644
--- a/src/rsz/src/Resizer.cc
+++ b/src/rsz/src/Resizer.cc
@@ -5474,7 +5474,15 @@ void Resizer::swapArithModules(int path_count,
 // Journal to roll back changes
 void Resizer::initForJournalRestore()
 {
-  init();
+  // init() also recounts the design area over every instance and drops
+  // the swappable-cell cache. After an ECO undo the netlist is back at the
+  // journal's checkpoint, so the area is the checkpoint's (the committer
+  // restores its snapshot) and the equivalent-cell lists, which depend on
+  // the libraries only, are still right. Levelization is what an undo can
+  // leave behind.
+  initBlock();
+  sta_->ensureLevelized();
+  graph_ = sta_->graph();
 }
 
 void Resizer::updateParasiticsAndTiming()
